# Docker resolves these patterns against the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT — this directory
# — and never against the Dockerfile's own directory. `projects/app/.dockerignore`
# and `projects/api/.dockerignore` are therefore NOT read when building from here
# (see docker-compose.yml: `context: .`). This file is the only one that applies.
#
# For the same reason a bare `node_modules` would only match `./node_modules`;
# every pattern that must also hit `projects/*` needs a `**/` glob. Both service
# Dockerfiles copy their sources straight out of this context
# (`COPY projects/app/ …`, `COPY . /tmp/src`), so what is listed below is exactly
# what keeps a developer's local artefacts and secrets out of the images.

# Dependencies — the images install their own from the lockfile
**/node_modules
**/.pnpm-store
**/.npm

# `.npmrc` is a token file by convention. The ROOT one has to stay: both deps
# stages do `COPY . /tmp/src` and then `cp -f /tmp/src/.npmrc /app/`, so excluding
# it would silently drop any registry auth the install needs. (The `cp` is
# `|| true`, so a missing file does not break the build — it just installs
# unauthenticated, which fails later and less legibly.) The nested copies are read
# by nothing: both installs run from /app.
# Written as an explicit path rather than `**/.npmrc` + `!.npmrc`, because a
# negation would also have to reason about re-inclusion under an excluded
# directory — more subtlety than one entry is worth.
projects/*/.npmrc

# Build output — a stale .output/dist must never be copied into an image.
# LOAD-BEARING, not hygiene: the CI `build` job publishes projects/app/.output as
# an artifact, and GitLab hands artifacts to later-stage jobs by default. These
# lines are what keeps a leftover build out of the image (see the stale-`.output`
# incident recorded in .gitlab-ci.yml's turboops-build header). `dependencies: []`
# on the deploy jobs is the second layer; do not remove either without the other.
**/.output
**/.nuxt
# The check chain's isolated Nuxt build dir (`NUXT_BUILD_DIR=.nuxt-check`), the
# `lt dev test` build dir, and its Nitro OUTPUT dir (`NITRO_OUTPUT_DIR=.output-test`,
# a separate axis — `**/.output` does not match it). All local-only and rewritten
# on every run. Mirrors projects/app/.gitignore, which Docker never reads from here.
**/.nuxt-check
**/.nuxt-test
**/.output-test
# Catch-all beneath the named entries. `NUXT_BUILD_DIR` / `NITRO_OUTPUT_DIR` make
# `.nuxt-<x>` / `.output-<x>` trivial to spawn, so enumerating names is structurally
# incomplete — a manual `.nuxt-perf` / `.output-perf` profiling run in projects/app was
# the first one nobody had an entry for. It reached the BUILDER stage through this
# file, and `COPY projects/app/` sits directly above the expensive `nuxt build` layer,
# so a stray local build dir busts the image cache. The named entries stay: they carry
# the reason each dir exists.
**/.nuxt-*
**/.output-*
**/.nitro
**/.cache
**/dist

# Secrets — `nuxt build` reads .env, so a local .env would silently change the
# bundle. `.env*` covers the whole family rather than enumerating shapes.
# `.env.example` goes with it: no build step reads it.
**/.env*

# VCS + editor + tooling
**/.git
**/.gitignore
**/.husky
**/.idea
**/.vscode
**/.claude
**/*.swp
**/*.swo

# Runtime state — written while the stack runs locally, never needed by a build.
# `.lt-dev` is `lt dev`'s directory (detached server logs + scratch state); the
# API logs every request it serves into it.
**/.lt-dev
# The TUS staging dir, i.e. real user documents. ANCHORED on purpose: a bare
# `**/uploads` matches any directory of that name at any depth, which would also
# swallow application source — a Nuxt route `app/pages/uploads/` or a component
# folder would silently vanish from the build context and the page would just not
# exist in the image. (`app/components/Upload/` already exists — a near miss.)
projects/api/uploads

# Test + report artefacts. A Playwright trace records the full request log of the
# failing test, session cookie included. Most of these are gitignored inside
# projects/app, so they never show up in `git status` to remind anyone they are
# there — note that at the MONOREPO ROOT only `coverage` and `*.log` are, so a
# stray root-level report directory is visible in git but still excluded here.
**/coverage
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
**/blob-report
**/*.log
# Rotated logs too: `**/*.log` only matches a segment ENDING in `.log`, so
# `.lt-dev/api.log.1` sails straight past it.
**/*.log.*

# Docker + CI metadata. Docker reads the Dockerfile out-of-band, so excluding it
# from the context is safe and merely keeps it out of the image layers.
**/Dockerfile*
**/docker-compose*
**/.dockerignore
.gitlab-ci.yml
.gitlab
**/.github
.turboops.json

# Misc
.DS_Store
**/*.tgz
